From: devvm <https://github.com/bindreams/devvm>
Subject: [PATCH] target/i386/whpx: take the XSAVE area size from the hypervisor

QEMU answers CPUID[0xD,0].EBX, the XSAVE area size for the enabled XCR0
features, from its own stale copy of XCR0. The hypervisor already supplies the
current value in DefaultResultRbx, which QEMU takes for sub-leaves 1 and 2. A
Windows guest that enables AVX then sees an area smaller than XSAVE writes (576
bytes instead of 2432), and software that sizes a buffer from it, such as
Cygwin, overruns it.

devvm-only; applies after 0002.
---
--- a/target/i386/whpx/whpx-all.c
+++ b/target/i386/whpx/whpx-all.c
@@ -2659,9 +2659,10 @@
                 }
             }
 
-            /* CPUID[0xD,{1,2}].EBX are dynamic depending on guest features. */
+            /* CPUID[0xD,{0,1,2}].EBX are dynamic, depending on XCR0 and XSS. */
             if (vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rax == 0xd) {
-                if (vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rcx == 1
+                if (vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rcx == 0
+                    || vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rcx == 1
                     || vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rcx == 2) {
                     reg_values[4].Reg64 = vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.DefaultResultRbx;
                 }
